I"Å<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Adverse childhood experiences, meaning highly stressful and potentially traumatic events in childhood or adolescence, are linked to higher risks of mental illness and other problems later in life. Studying them at scale needs a lot of text to learn from, but labelled examples are scarce.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We built an ontology-driven, self-supervised approach. It takes the output of basic language processing and learns concept representations with an auto-encoder, guided by a structured vocabulary of the concepts involved. The aim was a publicly available resource built from social media text.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>The method produces a resource that can support large-scale machine learning, such as training transformer-based language models, on social media data, without relying on large amounts of hand labelling.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Scarce labelled data is a common obstacle in health research. Using domain knowledge to supervise the AI is one way around it. The paper and code are openly available.</p>
